<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Title>Quartz.NET Plugins</Title>
    <Description>Quartz.NET Plugins - a schedule declared in XML or JSON and reloaded as the file changes, and job and trigger history logging; $(Description)</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);plugins;xml-configuration;json-configuration;job-history</PackageTags>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Quartz</RootNamespace>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>True</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!--
      The claim, and what it is worth - the same statement Quartz.csproj makes, and read the same way.
      IsAotCompatible turns on IsTrimmable and the trim, AOT and single-file analyzers, all of which were
      already on here (issue #3431); what it adds is the statement, so the four are spelled out beside it.

      What is proven: Quartz.Trimming.Canary references this package, is published by ILCompiler as a native
      executable on Windows, Linux and macOS on every pull request, and is run. It loads a quartz_jobs.json
      and a quartz_jobs.xml through UseJsonSchedulingConfiguration and UseXmlSchedulingConfiguration, each
      naming a job's type as text that the scheduler then resolves and fires, with UseStructuredJobLogging
      listening - the file read through metadata the compiler wrote, the XML through the schema Quartz
      embeds, and the job types kept by AddJobType<T>() in the application, which is what the how-to says
      to do.

      What a native publish reports against this package: nothing. Two IL2026 were recorded until 4.6, one
      per schedule-file plugin, for the job type the file spells as text; the processors said
      RequiresUnreferencedCode for it, and the plugins, implementing interfaces Quartz does not own both
      sides of, could not carry the attribute on. They say it no longer: a name is resolved from the types
      the application registered, and by reflection only while Quartz.TypeNameResolution.IsSupported is on,
      which a trimmed publish turns off (src/Quartz/FeatureSwitches.cs). There is no TrimAnalysisBaseline.cs
      beside this file any more, and a warning appearing here is a new one - fix it rather than starting a
      baseline for it; build/Build.cs fails the canary leg on one. It is trimming, not dynamic code: the
      package produces no IL3050 at all. The classic logging pair is not run by the canary; it logs through
      the same listeners as the structured pair and reflects over nothing.
    -->
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>true</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.Jobs\Quartz.Jobs.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>